preConditionHook:
// GRIMES'S ACCELERATING, PARABOLIC CLIMAX near the end of an extended trend,
// carried as an entry-side CONTEXT FLAG: the most recent leg has travelled
// more than accelMultiple times the leg before it, and the prior leg ran in
// the trade's own direction. Grimes glosses exhaustion as a synonym of climax.
//
// A ROLE TWIN OF ParabolicAccelerationExit, deliberately, and the arithmetic
// is that rule's term for term. Brooks is explicit (p376) that his reading is
// an EXIT and NOT a countertrend entry, while Grimes states the same
// measurement as a climax CONTEXT test; an Exit rule cannot sit in an entry
// pane, which is this corpus's standing reason for role twins (the
// ConsecutiveCloseExit / MomentumConsecutiveBars precedent). The book defaults
// differ too: 4 bars there, 5 here.
//
// THE PRIOR-LEG SIGN TERM IS LOAD-BEARING. Without it the comparison does not
// mean "steeper": against a NEGATIVE prior leg, any recent leg at all clears
// accelMultiple times it. The signed, mirrored form is what makes a bull
// climax fire the long side and a bear climax the short.
//
// THE TICK-COUNT HALF of the book's sentence ("usually accompanied by an increase
// in volume") is VolumeAboveAverageMultiple in a separate pane -- atomic beats
// composite. The candidate's AbsValue spelling, which covers both directions
// at once, lost to the signed form already on disk.
//
// Two fixed-offset net moves of equal length, so nothing is stateful and
// nothing reads the position. Differences of closes only, so the rule passes
// the shift test.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: 2 * legBars.
recentLeg = close[0] - close[legBars];
priorLeg = close[legBars] - close[2 * legBars];
